 <p><strong>With other users:</strong> your display name, bio, profile photo(s), and any optional details you choose to share (pronouns, gender, sexual orientation, interests) are visible to other users you've crossed paths with, once your photo has passed moderation. Your exact location is never shown to any other user.</p>
 <p><strong>"Interested" and local businesses (optional, off unless you turn it on):</strong> when you mark a public Gathering as Interested, that mark is private: no other user sees it, and the Gathering's host sees only how many people are interested, never who. Only if you turn on “Include my ‘Interested’ in anonymous local trends” in Settings, your Interested marks on public Gatherings from the last 14 days may be counted in an anonymous, area-level figure shown to nearby businesses in the same category (for example, "7 people nearby are interested in Coffee gatherings"). A figure is shown only when at least 5 different people are counted. Businesses see only the category and the count: never your name, profile, which Gathering, or any individual mark, and they cannot contact or target you because of it. You can turn this off at any time; you are then left out of these figures from that moment on. We keep a record of when you turned it on or off, until you delete your account.</p>
 <p><strong>With service providers:</strong> we use third-party services to operate the App, including a database and backend hosting provider (Supabase), an automated content moderation and image/text analysis provider (Amazon Web Services), an email delivery provider (Brevo), an SMS verification provider (Twilio), a product analytics provider (PostHog), a crash reporting provider (Sentry), and a payment processing provider (Apple, via in-app purchase, and RevenueCat for subscription management). These providers process data on our behalf and are not permitted to use it for their own purposes.</p>
+<!-- DRAFT 2026-11-26, NOT REVIEWED: owner + counsel approve before publishing (PRODUCT_AUDIT/PRIVACY_CORRECTIONS_DRAFT_2026-11-26.md) -->
+<p><strong>With our AI provider (Anthropic):</strong> some features send content to Anthropic, which provides the AI models we use. What is sent is only what that feature needs: the text, photo or video being checked when automated screening reviews a business offer, a business profile or a photo shared in a chat; the message being translated when you ask for a translation; what you type into an assistant (for example, describing a gathering or your business); and, when you ask for a conversation starter, an introduction or a summary of your strengths, the profile details that feature uses, such as interests, prompts, bio, profile answers you chose to share and, for the strengths summary, the artists in your favourite tracks; and, when you ask for help wording a message in a chat, only the kind of message you picked (for example, asking someone out), never the conversation. When you ask for an introduction, the other person's display name is included; otherwise no name is sent. We never send your email address, phone number, exact location or account identifier. Anthropic processes this content to provide the service to us and, under its commercial terms, does not use it to train its models. An automated screening result can be reviewed by a person at Nearby.</p>
+<p><strong>Bot protection (Cloudflare Turnstile):</strong> to protect sign-in from automated abuse, the sign-in screen runs Cloudflare Turnstile, which checks that a real person is signing in. Cloudflare receives your IP address and technical information about your device and browser for this check, under Cloudflare's privacy policy. It does not receive your phone number or account details.</p>
+<p><strong>Notifications (Expo):</strong> push notifications are delivered through Expo's notification service, which receives your device's notification token and the text of the notification being sent.</p>
+<p><strong>GIF search (GIPHY):</strong> when you search for a GIF in a chat, the words you search for are sent from your device to GIPHY, which also receives your IP address and technical information about the request, and GIFs are loaded from GIPHY.</p>
 <p><strong>With Google (Google Maps Platform):</strong> some features use Google Maps Platform services. When you use them, the App sends the information that feature needs directly from your device to Google:</p>
 <ul>
   <li><strong>Place search</strong> (for example, places nearby for an activity, or finding your business when you sign up as a business): your device's current location (the point the search is centered on), and the words of that search: a category or activity name (such as "Coffee"), or, for a search you type where Google place results are shown (such as a search in Discover, or your business's name), the text you typed.</li>
